A||||2||Ken Wittlief||rusty 'stator' inside the dist - reason enough to replace it?||||5||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||05-18-2001||03:29 PM||||My car is hesitating and sputtering and backfiring most of the time on the transistion from idle to about 20mph (not always - more after its warmed up).<P> I am pretty sure I have a stuck/leaky injector, cause i am getting o2 sensor rich errors when I stop for a light after driving at a steady speed for a while, so I figure the leaky/stuck injector is causing that (O2 sensor is new).<P>  But the 6 edged metal plate inside the distributor base is very rusty, and I know that is what the pickup coil senses the postition of - so:<P>  1. is being rusty enough of a reason to relace it?<P>  2. I need to get a new one from Pontiac? My favorite parts store says they are not in his books.<P>  3. How much damage ($$$) to the wallet am I looking at?<P>  I plan on pulling the injectors and checking for leaks or sputtery spray patterns this weekend - should I take care of the rich mixture problem first?<P>  The car never misses or runs rough at idle or at speed, it only sputters and backfires when accelerating from a stop. I figure it could be getting overly rich when idling, or it could be that 'stator' thing.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-18-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||My7Fieros||05-18-2001||03:49 PM||||Lets see if Im thinking about the same thing you're talking about.....<BR>the "crown" looking piece in the dist? the part that kind of goes around the pick up coil. If thats it, heres what I did. After pulling the dist (and of course replacing the pick up coil and ignition control mod) I took the dist apart. On the bottom of the shaft part of the dist, theres a small pin that you can tap out..this will allow the inner shaft part (including the 6 pointed "crown") to slide out.You may have to be patient and work at it though...sometimes it wont come out all the way due to crud build up in it...just keep working at it, it'll eventually slide all the way out. I then cleaned it and took it to a hardware store that a friend of mine owns. I used his key cutting machine...because theres a rotating wire brush on it, to clean the 6 pointed part. It shines like new now! Took the rust right off. Now obvioulsy everybody dosnt have access to this machine...so, put a wire wheel on the end of a drill and use it to clean this part. <BR>After I did that, I lubed the inner shaft part, then put the dist back together. Like mentioned above, I replaced the ignition control mod, pick up coil, cap, and rotor.<BR>This made a TREMENDOUS difference. Try it out. Of course if you're talking about something completely different....sorry!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Good luck, M7F<p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 05-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||05-18-2001||03:52 PM||||Ken, Everyone I,ve ever seen is rusted, that in it's self means nothing. <BR>Just make sure they are not making physical contact due to a worn shaft.||||reg||1
